ople get programs such as st. Jude  Children's Research Hospital one of the  areas they started with was pediatric al  why because that was a huge need at  their institution they would stream a  lot of patients with pediatric al and  that's where they had expertise so they  went after that one versus one they  understood the vets if we will get the  energy of Florida their cardiologists  were really interested in to see 19 in  clopidogrel and cardiology  pharmacogenomics so they started there  first but if we will get Northshore well  we didn't start with pediatric al  because that population didn't exist for  us we talked to the cardiologists and  they weren't sold on the clinical data  available at the time first comes from  Devlin pharmacogenomics oh we didn't go  after that but instead our psychiatrists  really interested in understanding how  do we use pharmacogenomic so we went  after them first on the clinical  implementation side but because the  genetics around depression in psychiatry  is complicated it's multiple genes we  didn't actually systematically support  that first we were supporting that  through clinical consultations and we  went after actually the therapure  earrings and TPMT from  rheumatological standpoint first because  that logic was well established and it  was an easy way of building in an epoch  i'm our electronic medical record so  make sure you took two different  approaches but identifying what  resources we had who was excited and  where we thought we could get some major  momentum and then we began to grow those  together so that we began to have  overlap between the two so it's really  important to understand where are your  resources who is available who wants to  work with you on pharmacogenomics what  patient population do you have that can  benefit from this and pick the one  that's right for you unfortunately I  don't have the answer for this is the  right one to go to every time but  there's probably no one wrong answer  here there are a lot of right answers  for you to go after so thanks for that  question  our next question is what is the biggest  challenge facing your program today  um so the biggest challenge facing our  program today  today I'm not answered is one challenge  I'm gonna answer it as multiple  challenges because I think on each front  we we have different challenges so one  is we've done a lot of work in  Northshore on getting this data to the  provider and we need to better  understand how do we get this data to  the patient patients are probably the  best vehicle for disseminating this data  across one institution as well as  multiple institutions and Northshore we  have a number of patients that receive  care at multiple health systems and  those other health systems don't have  the informatics support that we have in  North Shore but those patients should  still be able to benefit from the  pharmacogenomic testing that we have  done and so we have to figure out how do  we deliver this data to patients in a  way in which they can understand it in a  way which they can understand it and  then educate their other providers about  it so we're trying to figure out what  that looks like it's probably a patient  based portal on patient facing portal  with a lot of information  another challenge that we have is as  we've developed pharmacogenomics other  genomics have become important so a  retic Airy cancer genomics risk for  cardiomyopathy sudden cardiac death  that's becoming an area of interest at  our healthcare system so we're trying to  understand how do we integrate all of  that so that clinicians have one genomic  place to go as opposed to having a place  to go for this genomic or that genomics  or those genomics because we think if we  do that ultimately they're going to lose  interest in genomics and that brings me  to the third challenge that we're facing  today  that's going to be keeping up interest  in pharmacogenomics for those providers  who have not already adopted  pharmacogenomics so when the tools are  new and shiny it's pretty easy to  people's attention but you can see it  with any implementation not just  pharmacogenomics over time clinicians a  number of them lose interest and stop  using the tools a YouthBuild once you  have to go in and do the education in  addition to doing that re-education you  have to begin to understand why didn't  they lose interest what didn't we do  right that first time and so having a  system of  evaluating and improving and  implementing and evaluating and  improving and implementing over time  that that quality of improvement system  that we've learned so much about through  medical errors and other parts of  clinical implementation is also true  here so taking your tools and making  them better every time to meet the needs  of your provider because if you don't  need the needs of your providers why are  you doing implementation so those are  really three areas that were challenged  with today so again how do we deliver  this information to patients so it's  actually another user of our data  and also how are we going to get  providers onboard who are not already on  board so thanks for that question  and it looks like we have time for one  more class what pharmacogenetic tests do  you use in your institution  yeah so this pharmacogenomic test that  we use our institution it's a 20 gene  panel what's that about a hundred and  twenty different variants and it's a  panel that we customized for our own  institution we've run it in our in-house  laboratory and this is a good approach  for us partly because we have the  resources in the laboratory space but  when we originally started we did not  use this test we actually use some  third-party testing um it's probably a  question that I can answer there a whole  another presentation I'll spare you  those details today but  identifying a pharmacogenomic test that  meets the needs of your patients and  providers is incredibly important what I  mean by needs I mean testing the genes  and variants that are important for  whatever drugs you're going to do the  patient whatever clinical area that  you're going to implement on as well as  testing the variants that are found in  the ethnic mix of your population is  going to be important as well otherwise  you may get some erroneous answers you  can look to add warfarin  pharmacogenomics to see the importance  of testing variants that are seen in the  ethnic mix of your population um  there are a number of tests out there in  the commercial space having a thorough  way of evaluating them and making sure  they're meeting the needs of your health  system going to be incredibly important  you do this having a systematic approach  is incredibly important there are some  papers on the literature that can help  you go through this process  it's a really fundamental question you  have to answer early on again it's one  of those ones where there's multiple  answers out there for Northshore the  right idea for us was to do it ourselves  but I know for another a number of other  institutions that's not the right  approach so don't go to it because you  think it's on the best thing to do  unless you have the support to do it  there are a number of great third-party  labs out there  right thanks for that question as well  and I think that concludes today so  thank you for your attention and I look  forward to seeing you guys success in  the future with implementing  pharmacogenomics I  would like to once again thank dr. John  inverter for his presentation I would  also like to thank lab rats were making  today's educational webcast possible  before we go I want to let everyone know  that today's webcast will be available  for on-demand viewing through May of  2018 you will receive an email from  labyrinth letting you know when this  webcast will be available for replay  please share that announcement with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event 